WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to the
Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:06.020
where we discuss science and science
-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.720
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor of neurobiology

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:14.500
and ophthalmology
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.640
Today, we are discussing hair.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.300
Hair is a topic that occupies
the minds of many people.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.480
There are people that are losing their

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:26.820
hair and want to halt or
reverse that loss of hair.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.560
Today, we will talk about all the ways

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.920
that science has taught us we can
slow or even reverse hair loss.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.080
I confess that researching today's topic

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.800
was a particular joy for me not because
I'm obsessed with hair,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.360
mine or the hair of others,
but because hair turns out to be

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.920
fascinating from the perspective
of cellular biology and stem cells,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.840
which is a topic that I've long been
interested in and that for much

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.380
of my career I focused on in
the context of development.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.280
When your brain and your nervous system
develop, it develops from a small batch

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.300
of cells  that turns
into many trillions of cells.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:06.130
It does that by cell replication,
something that we call the cell cycle.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.440
We'll talk a little bit about
this and so-called mitosis today.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.440
I promise not to get into too much detail.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.840
But what makes hair so very interesting
from a biological standpoint is that every

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.880
hair, every single individual strand
of hair has its own little stem cell

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:26.040
niche, meaning its own little pocket down
there in the follicle in which specific

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:31.080
stem cells give rise to those hairs
for different durations of time,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.260
depending on the hair,
where it is on your body, et cetera.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.360
For instance, the hairs on your head will

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.660
undergo ongoing growth
for 4-6 or even 8 years.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.080
Were you to not cut your hair,
it would continue to grow.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.400
One single hair would continue to grow.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:53.020
I guess we could say all the hairs will
continue to grow for up to 8 years.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:58.400
That is very different from, for instance,
your eyebrows, which have a much  shorter

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.240
period of hair growth lasting
on the order of months.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.880
That's why you don't see people with
eyebrows that extend down to their waist.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.360
But you can see people with hair on their

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.730
head that extends down to their
waist if they don't cut it.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.120
Now, that discrepancy illustrates for us

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.720
just how incredible hair follicles
and the stem cells that reside within

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.920
hairs are and their enormous potential
to give rise to these things that we call

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.020
hairs, which are simply proteins
of varying length.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.640
Today, we are going to address what

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:33.280
determines the length of a hair or rather
what determines how long a hair continues

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.520
to grow before it ceases growing
and eventually falls out.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.000
We're going to talk about what regulates
those stem cells,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.480
what allows them to continue to produce
hair or cease producing hair.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:44.840
As we do that,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.280
you will learn all the biology in clear,
simple terms,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.000
regardless of your background,
that will really set the stage

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:57.360
for understanding what we'll also talk
about, which is how to slow hair loss or

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.220
halt hair loss entirely  or
even reverse hair loss.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.280
We will talk about hormone -related
hair loss in both men and women.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.200
We will talk about some of the mechanical

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.610
and stress -related
influences on hair loss.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.520
We will talk about the chemical

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.920
and mechanical approaches to halting
and reversing hair loss,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:19.160
everything from minoxidil to
dutasteride to ketoconazole

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:24.360
to microneedling to thyroid,
estrogen, IGF-1 pathways.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.520
Again, all made very clear regardless

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.360
of whether or not you have
a background in biology or not.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.200
I will also dispel some of the common
myths about balding and hair replacement.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.920
If you've heard, for instance,
that you inherit your patterns of balding

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.000
from your mother's father,
that is not true.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.640
Although it is true that you do inherit
certain genes that influence whether or

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.980
not you have a predisposition to balding
in particular parts of your head,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.320
and believe it or not,
even in particular parts of your body.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.520
But it is not the case that you can simply

00:03:55.550 --> 00:04:01.480
find a photo of your mother's father, say,
age 50, or age 60 or 75,

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.500
and determine whether or not you'll have
the exact same pattern of hair loss.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.740
That's a myth that I'd like
to dispel right here and now.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.000
I will dispel some of the other myths

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:13.480
about hair loss, hair replacement,
and hair regrowth as well.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.320
Before we begin, I'd like  to emphasize

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.680
that this podcast is separate from my
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:19.520
It is, however,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.440
part of my desire and effort to bring zero
cost to consumer information about science

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.680
and science-related tools
to the general public.

