00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.100
Okay, let's talk about hair.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.260
In researching this episode by talking

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:13.000
to experts in the biology of hair,
and the stem cells that exist in all of us

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:17.240
that give rise to our hair growth,
and the pigmentation in our hair,

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:22.840
and in talking to experts who understand
how to halt and even reverse hair loss,

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.000
that there is a tremendously
interesting biology surrounding hair.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:30.900
But there's also an incredible
psychology around hair.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:34.000
In fact, most people who experience even

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.520
marginal hair loss undergo
pretty severe anxiety.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.880
Now, I confess this is not
something I can relate to.

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:42.140
I am losing my hair in certain places.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:43.200
I'm 47 years old.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.600
I've got a couple of patches up front
where there's very minimal hair.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.040
I think that, as we'll later learn in this

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:54.360
episode, reflects a higher density of DHT,
dihydrotestosterone, receptors

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.280
at that particular location  as
opposed to elsewhere in my scalp.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:01.100
But keeping my hair is not something
that I've fretted about much of my life.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:05.240
Yet as I was researching this episode,
I remembered an anecdote from my childhood

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:10.800
where my father told me,
and I think it was because I was stressing

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:14.040
about something,
I was trying to get to sleep, and he said,

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.500
"Don't stress . Calm
down." And here's why.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:21.760
If you stress too much,
it can actually make your hair fall out.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.480
In fact, I have a cousin who lay down one
night stressed,

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:29.780
and woke up the next morning , and
all of his hair was on his pillow.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.600
I'll never forget that story.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.160
I think he was trying
to get me to stress less.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:37.380
I don't know if that story
made me stress less or not.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.400
But in any event, I don't
know that that story is true.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.800
I'm not going to challenge
the authenticity of that story.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:47.160
I didn't have a chance to reach out
to my father and ask him to verify or not.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.760
But as we will soon learn,

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:55.000
it is true that our psychological
well-being can impact both the coloration

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.120
,, or lack thereof and the
growth rates of our hair.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:09:59.980
That's a real thing.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.400
The reverse is also true,

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:06.520
which is that as hair starts to thin, or
fall out, or change color,

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:10.200
many people expect intense
anxiety or even depression.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:12.900
This was not something
I was really aware of.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.400
Perhaps that's just because I've always
kept my hair pretty short anyway.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:16.680
I always assumed that if my hair started

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.320
to really fall out,
I would just shaved my head.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.720
But that's me, and that's
not most people out there.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:25.260
I think most people would
loathe to lose their hair.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.600
In fact, given the enormous number,

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:34.600
probably up in the high billions
of dollars and euros and other currency,

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:38.640
of course, that people invest in trying
to halt or reverse their hair loss,

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:41.220
it's clear that hair is
very important to people.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:46.640
What we know is that by age 50,
approximately 50% of all men and women

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:51.240
will have experienced significant enough
hair loss that they start to notice it.

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.240
And a large percentage,

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:59.720
up to 85% of those people will experience
some anxiety  that leads them to go out

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.400
and try and either halt or
reverse that hair loss.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.800
Now, why at age 50?

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:09.080
Well, an important point arises from that,
which is that the hair loss is not

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.580
occurring between the 49th
and 50th birthday.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:16.880
Hair loss is ongoing
from about age 30 to age 50.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.800
It's only by age 50, however,

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:23.660
that about 50% of people out there
start to notice that hair loss.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.060
This is typically because

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:29.000
they'll be in a bathroom or looking
in a mirror and the lighting will be

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:32.900
bright enough that it permeates
the outer boundary of their hair.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.600
They'll notice that their hair is
thinning in a particular location.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:37.860
That's usually how this thing happens.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:43.600
Again, our psychological states can impact
our patterns of hair growth or loss

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.640
and of course, patterns of hair growth,
but more typically,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:50.100
hair loss and hair graying can
really impact psychological states.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:53.700
This is a subject that people
take intense interest in.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:55.720
Today, we're going to talk about how hair

00:11:55.750 --> 00:12:02.460
normally grows, why it grows at the rate
, and for the duration that it happens to.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.500
Then as we talk about ways to intervene
with that hair loss,

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.960
those biological mechanisms will come up
because they really provide a nice

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:14.560
framework for explaining why certain
treatments  work more or less well or why

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.520
certain treatments might have certain side
effects or total lack of side effects.

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:20.760
It will also highlight a really key theme

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.280
that will come up several times in today's
podcast, which is that there are both

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:30.300
mechanical and chemical approaches
to slowing and reversing hair loss.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.720
Mechanical approaches would be things  as

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:37.640
simple as massaging the scalp,
but mechanical changes to the scalp  can

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:41.000
cause either hair loss or
facilitate hair growth.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.040
This is why things like microneedling  are

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:46.220
so prominent in the context
of trying to reverse hair loss.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.880
But again, there are also chemical

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.260
approaches to trying to halt
or reverse hair loss.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:56.000
This relates to the fact that the hair
growth itself is strongly regulated

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:59.520
by hormones such as estrogen,
thyroid hormone,

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:03.680
insulin-like growth factor,
and that other hormones, in particular

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.560
the androgens,
so things like testosterone,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:11.720
but mainly it's derivatives like
dihydrotestosterone are very much involved

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:16.880
in setting the stage for hair growth
by controlling how big or small that pool

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:20.100
of stem cells that gives rise
to hair growth is.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.600
If any of the terms I just use are

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.240
confusing to you, don't worry, I will
make all of those very clear in a moment.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:28.500
It's actually all pretty
straightforward and simple.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.920
I'd like to just start by talking about

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:37.680
what hair is, how it grows, why it stops
growing, and why hair normally falls out.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.740
Let's talk about the biology of hair.

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:42.380
In doing so, I also want
to talk about stem cells.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.680
Now, keep in mind that when you hear

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.840
the word stem cells, you probably,
like most people, think about the sorts

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:51.360
of cells that people are getting injected
into their face to get rid of wrinkles,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.680
or give them new skin,
or to give them more hair  if it's

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:58.860
injected in the scalp, or into a joint
to repair a joint or a muscle.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:02.120
Those stem cells are what we
call exogenous stem cells.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:06.100
Exogenous, meaning from outside the body.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:09.480
The stem cells that we're going to talk
about today are so-called endogenous stem

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:12.900
cell, cells that we all make
that can give rise to other cells.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.940
That's really the definition
of a stem cell.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:18.500
Stem cells are present in all of us
from the very beginning of life.

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:21.800
When sperm meets egg, that cell,

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.200
which we think of as the egg,
starts to duplicate,

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:27.420
it incorporates the DNA from the sperm
and the egg, of course,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:29.960
starts to duplicate,
and then those cells give rise to more

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.080
cells and more cells and the ability
of all those cells to replicate and create

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:36.340
more cells are because those
cells  really are stem cells.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:41.980
Now, at some point we are a completed
body plan, as the biologists say.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:47.360
We end up with a brain, and a spinal cord,
and limbs, and fingers, and livers,

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.280
and guts, and all the things that we need
in order to be a functioning human being.

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:53.440
Even though we're a baby at that time,
we haven't grown up,

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:57.180
we have all the bits that we're
going to have for our entire life.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:01.520
At that point, many of the stem
cell populations disappear.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:04.380
For instance, past puberty,

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:08.320
and probably earlier, you don't
get many more new brain cells.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:11.960
You get a few, but you don't get many more
new brain cells because the brain doesn't

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:16.640
have many stem cell populations,
whereas other organs in your body maintain

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:21.200
little pockets of stem cells,
or in some cases, many stem cells that can

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:24.780
give rise to more and more
of that tissue across the lifespan.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:27.100
Hair is one such tissue.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:30.280
If we take a look at hair,
what we find is that, indeed,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:33.720
there are these things that we call hairs,
but there are also stem cells,

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:38.080
and those stem cells are actually what
give rise to the hairs that we see

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:42.780
on the head of our scalp or that we
see on the surface of our body.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:43.800
Right off the bat,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:48.120
you should know  that every single hair
that you have is there because you have

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:53.060
a stem cell population that is giving
rise  to that particular hair.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.240
Let's take a step back, or rather,

00:15:55.270 --> 00:16:00.480
I should say, let's zoom in on one hair
and the stem cell population that gives

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.400
rise to that hair, because in doing so,
you're going to learn  all the different

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:10.200
components that you can tap into if your
goal is to halt the loss of hairs or

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:13.620
to replace hairs  that have
already been lost.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:16.080
If we were to just zoom in at the level

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:21.000
of one hair, what you would find is
that that hair has what typically is

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:24.260
called a hair root, so that's
the portion below the skin.

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:25.280
When we say below the skin,

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:30.720
it means that it dives down into a narrow
trench, which is in the so-called

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:33.240
epidermis, which is this
outer layer of skin.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:35.040
It also has a shaft.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:38.460
The shaft is the part
that grows out above the skin.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:41.600
What you see on somebody's head
or you see on their arm,

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:44.600
or when you see an eyebrow,
you're seeing the shaft of the hair.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:47.740
The root, of course, goes below the skin.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:51.240
What most people don't realize, however,
is that down at the base of the root,

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:54.540
there's actually a little
cave, a little pocket.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:56.960
If you were to look at this,
it would look like a little bulb,

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:01.980
a little round area with a bunch
of stuff in it right below the root.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:05.360
Within that little cave,
there are stem cells.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:10.010
There are populations of cells
that have the ability to divide.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.330
We call this mitosis.

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:13.040
It's a process by which cells can actually

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:17.400
divide, and take DNA with them,
and then give rise to other cells.

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:21.760
We call those cells that divide and move
out, we call those daughter cells.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.800
We call the cells that give rise to them
progenitor cells, but they are effectively

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:29.520
stem cells  that give rise to these
what we call daughter cells.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.920
Those daughter cells then become

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:35.740
the various types of cells
that make up the hair.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.240
When you see a hair,

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:40.240
you're not seeing something
that grows throughout the lifespan.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.240
You're seeing something that's going to be

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:46.240
born down there in that little cave,
then is going to grow.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:48.700
It's actually going to stack
up on top of itself.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.480
That's because hairs are made
up of a protein called keratin.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:52.440
There are a bunch of different kinds

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.960
of keratin depending on what
kind of hair you're looking at.

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:58.320
But these are little proteins that stack
up on top of one another,

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:01.680
and they're structured in a way
that makes them pretty darn durable.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.280
I mean, it's possible, of course,
to pull a hair out.

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.940
But if you've ever tried to tear a hair,
in particular,

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:10.680
thick hair like one from the face or even
one off the top of your head,

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.860
it's actually a pretty
tensile strong little thing.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.040
That's because keratins  stack up on top

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:20.140
of one another and bind to one
another with a really strong bond.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:21.760
What you end up with is a bunch

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:25.980
of proteins stacked up on top of one
another, and that's the actual hair.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:27.840
We've got the hair shaft, the hair root,

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:30.840
and then we've got the stem cells down
there in that pocket  that give rise

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:33.880
to the various cells
that make up the actual hair.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:35.600
We also have down there  in that little

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.040
cave, which, by the way,
is actually called the hair bulb,

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.290
if you really want to know the technical
name because it's shaped like a bulb.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:45.520
We have not just stem cells,
but we have cells that give rise

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:49.600
to the pigment of the hair
that create what's called melanin.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:51.480
Now, some people have very blond hair,

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:54.220
very light hair,
some people have darker hair.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:56.760
But everybody, unless they have what's

00:18:56.790 --> 00:19:00.440
called the albino mutation,
where the hairs are truly white,

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:03.400
they lack all melanin,
and it's a pretty rare condition,

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:06.560
although it does happen,
most people have some degree of melanin

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:11.240
in their hairs because there are little
pockets of melanin-producing cells.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.600
Melanin is just a protein that essentially

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:18.770
gets injected  into the keratin, into
the hair, and gives it its darker color.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:20.360
Now, there are a couple  other components

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:23.600
about the hair that you need to know
about, especially if you're interested

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.770
in reversing hair loss or
reversing graying of hair.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:30.530
One of those components is a little gland.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:34.920
Next to every hair root
within the dermal layer of the skin,

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:39.520
so this is below the epidermis,
there is a gland called the sebaceous

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:44.090
gland, and the sebaceous gland makes oily
stuff, and the oily stuff is called sebum.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:46.480
I know the name evokes something gross,

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:49.000
but sebum is actually really
cool and really important.

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:53.080
The sebum gets injected, or seeps rather,

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:58.240
into the area right around the hair
as the hair starts to approach the surface

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:01.860
where it goes from essentially
root to shaft.

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:03.420
The sebum does two things.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.200
First of all, it forms a little bit

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:08.640
of a seal right at the place
where the hair exits the skin.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:12.880
And that seal is very important,
actually, for waterproofing of your skin.

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:17.160
So we don't often think of ourselves as
waterproof because we are so accustomed

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.160
to water just landing on our
skin and and rolling off.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:23.000
But that's because of some
of the oily properties of our skin.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:26.080
Now, it's also true that our skin is
pretty densely packed with cells.

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:31.040
But in the absence of sebum,
we would not be as waterproof as we are.

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.480
Now as I mentioned, sebum has
two important properties.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:35.640
The other important property of sebum is

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:41.050
that it actually is a strong
antibacterial and antimicrobial.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.480
Most people don't realize this,
the oils of your skin provide a lot

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:47.480
of immune boundary,
so that things don't get  into the hair

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:50.640
root or the region around
it and infect our skin.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:55.040
So sebum, while the name is sort
of unattractive, to be honest,

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:59.280
is actually performing some essential
roles both for waterproofing and for our

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:02.480
immune system function,  protecting
us from various kinds of infection.

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.680
We're going to return to sebum later.
As it turns out,

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:10.200
sebum is also very important as it relates
to psoriasis and as it relates to some

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:13.570
of the fungal components
that can cause hair loss.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.290
So I'm just going to file that away.

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:16.840
There's another important component

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:21.040
of the region around hairs,
which is the  arrector pili muscle.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:22.960
The arrector pili muscle is a muscle

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:27.240
that lies diagonally between that bulb
portion of the hair or a little bit above

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:29.560
it, and goes up to the
surface of the skin.

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:31.500
The arrector pili muscle

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:37.360
is a muscle that contracts when we
get cold or when we get scared.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:42.080
So if you've ever had goosebumps,
that's because the  arrector pili muscle

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:47.600
contracts pulling the skin at the surface
down around the little hair follicles,

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:50.760
or at least where the hairs
meet the surface of the skin.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:54.280
And so those little bumps are actually
where little micro hairs reside.

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:56.640
And the dimples between them are

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:00.840
the dimples that occur when this
arrector pili muscle pulls down.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:02.660
Now, why would this muscle exist?

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.200
It has a couple of important functions.

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:06.120
One of the functions is that when it

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:11.760
pulls down, it causes, as the name
suggests, the hairs to stand up.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.000
Maybe not perfectly vertical,

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.280
but when you hear, oh, I was so
frightened, my hair stood up on end.

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:18.720
And that's because
the hair has become erect.

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:20.020
They stand up.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.160
Now, why would this
happen when we get cold?

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:24.600
It happens because when the hairs stand

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:29.700
up, air can be trapped between those
hairs and can actually warm our body.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:34.080
This is not so much the case if you
have very light hair on your skin.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.520
If you're a very hairy person,

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:38.280
this is going to be a more robust
aspect of your physiology.

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:43.440
And yes, this is why dogs like Huskies can
go out in the snow and still remain warm.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:44.520
When they get cold,

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:48.940
their hairs actually stand up a bit on end
because of the contraction of these

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:51.000
arrector pili muscle
trapping air in there.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.600
And then their body warms the hair
trapped beneath the hair.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.040
And it's like they've got  a blanket

00:22:55.070 --> 00:23:00.360
on made by the interface between the hair,
the air, and their skin.

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:02.360
So just to recap all the components

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:05.520
of hair and the different things around it
that are going to be relevant

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:08.560
for understanding how
to replace hair that's lost.

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:14.160
We have the hair itself,
which has the shaft that sticks out over

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.400
the skin, goes a little bit into the skin,
but basically sticks out over the skin.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:21.360
We have the root portion
which goes down into the skin.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.520
It goes through the epidermis
and into the dermis.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.200
Then we have this bulb like
region down at the bottom.

00:23:26.230 --> 00:23:30.200
Down at the bottom of that bulb,
we have stem cells that actually give rise

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:34.500
to the actual hair, and we have
pigmented cells that pigment that hair.

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:40.720
In addition, and this is very important,
there are capillaries that go

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:46.520
into that bulb region down at the bottom
of the hair and that can serve and support

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:51.090
the stem cells, the melanin producing
cells, which are called melanocytes.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:52.800
So the melanin producing cells in the stem

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:58.160
cells get a lot of blood flow that allows
them to keep providing new hair or

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:02.560
the proteins that make up hair
and the pigment that goes into those hairs

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:07.800
and those little capillaries deliver not
just nutrients and things of that sort,

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:11.320
but they also deliver oxygen because it
turns out that the whole process

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:14.200
of growing more hair is
a very active process.

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:16.280
Now, as soon as you hear oxygen and you

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:21.240
hear that the growth is an active process,
that should cue you to why so many

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:26.320
of the stories around how to keep your
hair and regrow hair involve statements

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:29.040
like don't wear a hat,
it'll make your hair fall out.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.740
Or if you want your hair to grow back,

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:34.360
don't wear hats or massage your
scalp or increase blood flow.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.480
Or why some people will suggest

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:38.840
that people take peppermint oil,
for instance,

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:42.960
or menthol type oils of different
kinds and massage them into the scalp.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:44.520
Things that make the scalp tingle.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:47.640
Or there will be light
therapies designed to what?

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:51.040
To increase blood flow to the scalp.

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.040
The whole rationale there is that you're

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:57.000
trying to increase blood flow to the stem
cell and the melanocytes populations

00:24:57.030 --> 00:25:00.000
that support the hairs
and that actually create the hairs.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.330
Now, whether or not those are  approaches
work we'll touch  on a little bit later.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:04.880
I'll just give you a little bit of a hint

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:10.280
right now, which is that while no single
one of those approaches that I described

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:16.840
is known to regrow hair in a very robust
way because of the requirement for oxygen

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:20.880
and nutrients and because it's such
an active process for the stem cells

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:25.260
and melanocytes to grow and darken
the hairs that grow out of your skin.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:30.760
It is true that manipulations or
treatments that increase blood flow

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:37.680
to those regions can at least slow
the loss of hair or can even extend

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:40.810
the duration over which
hairs continue to grow.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:44.040
So if you've heard things like don't wear
a hat, if you want to maintain your hair.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.960
Or massage your scalp,
if you want your hair to grow faster.

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:51.120
In some sense that's true,
but none of those manipulations on their

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:55.880
own is going to robustly enhance
the rate of of your hair growth.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:57.320
Those things are designed to be done

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:02.000
in conjunction with some other treatments
that have been shown in many,

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:05.560
many clinical studies to increase
the rate and duration of hair growth.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.120
So now you have in mind a picture

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:09.480
of what's happening at the level
of individual hairs.

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.800
And if you're anything like me,
you're probably thinking, Wow,

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:15.760
there's a lot going on down there
just below the surface of the skin.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:16.720
And indeed there is.

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:19.720
But really the things to think about are
that stem cell population that actually

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.500
give rise to the hair proteins so
that actually create the hair.

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:27.020
The melanocytes that darken that hair,
that give it pigment.

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:33.020
That sebaceous gland and the oil sebum
that provides some important antimicrobial

00:26:33.050 --> 00:26:35.560
and other properties
to that general region.

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:38.800
And that arrector pili muscle,
that arrector pili muscle,

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:42.480
as I mentioned earlier,
is important for creating goosebumps

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:45.720
and it's important for keeping
Huskies warm in cold environments.

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.440
But it's doing some other really important

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:51.940
things as well, and we'll talk about
those as time goes on in this episode.

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:54.800
Right now, what I want to do is just talk

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:59.840
for a moment about how hairs actually
grow and why they grow the way they do.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:01.920
This is extremely important toward

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:07.140
understanding hair replacement
and slowing hair loss.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:11.090
There are three basic
phases of growth of a hair.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:13.160
The first phase is the phase

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:18.560
in which the stem cells down in that bulb
give rise to the cells that make up

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:21.560
the proteins of the hair,
so the actual growth of the hair.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:26.560
And keep in mind that the hair is
actually growing from the bottom up.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.400
Now, you might think, of course,
it's growing from the bottom.

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:29.440
Everyone knows that.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:30.840
But a lot of people think that the hair

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:32.800
starts growing right
at the surface of the skin.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:33.760
That's not the way it works.

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:37.720
The hair is actually growing from deep
within the root and stacking up and then

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:42.180
eventually extends out
across the top of the skin.

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:46.570
That growth phase is
called the anagen phase.

00:27:46.600 --> 00:27:47.920
A-N-A-G-E-N.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:52.960
And this for some people will  ring a bell
because if you've ever been interested

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:55.880
in weightlifting or even if you're
an endurance runner,

00:27:55.910 --> 00:28:01.480
you'll hear about things that are anabolic
that promote growth, so Anna of Growth.

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:04.680
Or catabolic  that promote breakdown.

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:07.040
So the first phase of hair growth is

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:11.480
called the anagen phase,
and it's a period of varying duration

00:28:11.510 --> 00:28:14.920
depending on which hair
in the body we're talking about.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:17.000
So the anagen or the growth phase

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:21.720
for hairs on the head, as I mentioned
earlier, is anywhere from 2 to 8 years.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.880
For most people, it's going
to be about six years.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:28.780
What this means is that if we were to just
not cut our hair, just let our hair grow

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:33.160
for 2 to 8 years,
that hair would eventually grow

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:38.080
to a length that it was at its
maximum and then would stop growing.

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:43.740
So we can say that the anagen phase
of hairs on the scalp is 2 to 8 years.

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:46.220
The the duration of the growth phase.

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:47.960
Contrast that, for instance,

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:52.720
with the duration of the anagen
phase for hairs of the eyebrows.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:59.080
The hairs of the eyebrows grow
about 4.2mm per month.

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:00.760
Believe it or not, people
have measured this thing.

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:01.760
Now that's an average.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:07.330
So some people are going to have eyebrows
that grow much longer per month.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:11.800
I'm somebody who, for instance,
has mostly the same length eyebrow hairs,

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:14.640
but every once in a while I get one
of those eyebrow hairs that really seems

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.880
to be heading off my head,
like really wants out of there.

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:18.760
And so it's much longer than the rest.

00:29:18.760 --> 00:29:19.760
What does that mean?

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:21.740
Does it mean that it grew faster?

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:22.840
Maybe.

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:29.320
But chances are the stem cell population
in that particular eyebrow follicle

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:34.960
for that one eyebrow hair
is longer than it is for the others.

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:35.960
This is really important.

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:38.360
I'm trying to illustrate two
principles at once here.

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:41.920
The first principle is that different
hairs on your body,

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:45.280
including the hairs on your scalp, have
a growth phase of different duration.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:48.160
This is why the hairs on your head can
grow very, very long because they have

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:52.440
a very long anagen or growth phase,
and the hairs on your eyebrows will only

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:56.680
grow for a few months  before they
actually fall out and then have

00:29:56.710 --> 00:30:03.290
to undergo replication  of the stem cells
to give you new eyebrow hair to then grow.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:06.920
What's important here is not just
that there are differences in the duration

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:11.240
of the growth phase,
but rather that the rate of hair growth

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:16.700
is not something that tends to differ
within a given body region.

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:18.040
You'll hear people say, "Oh,

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:20.640
my hair grows really,
really fast." Other people will say, "Oh,

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:25.120
my hair grows really, really slowly.'
That is probably not the case.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:27.480
Well, there could be slight
differences in the rate of growth.

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.720
That is the addition of more  keratin

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:35.220
to the actual hairs,
so creation of more hair more quickly.

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:38.960
Almost certainly what's happening is
that the duration of the anagen phase

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:43.330
in some people is just much longer
than it is in other people.

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:46.480
We don't realize this,
and we tend to think more  in terms of how

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:49.040
fast hair  grows,
because if you were to just look

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:52.240
at somebody's hair, you'd say, "Oh,
they're all more or less the same length."

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:54.800
I mean, some people's bangs are shorter
than the back because they cut them.

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:57.200
But if they were to just grow their hair,
you'd say, "Oh, it's all more or less

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.840
the same length." But if we were to zoom
in with a microscope,

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:03.560
we would see that there are a lot
of hairs down there in between the other

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:07.020
hairs that are very,
very short or even tiny, tiny, tiny.

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:10.940
Those are coming in as the other ones
are finishing their anagen phase.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.720
That's the anagen or growth phase.

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:17.050
After the anagen phase
comes the catagen phase.

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:21.120
Again, this resembles the word
catabolic or the breakdown phase.

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:26.240
During the catagen phase,
the hair is actually receding not

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:31.600
from the top down to the skin surface and
then into the root,  but the other way.

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:36.500
It's actually receding   from that bulb
region up toward the surface.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:39.280
That catagen phase is going to also be

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.620
of different duration depending
on which area of the body you're in.

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:44.960
It will vary a little bit  depending on

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:46.800
who you are, meaning
from person to person.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:50.280
We'll talk about the influences on the
anagen and catagen phase in a moment.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:53.800
Why is it important that it actually
recedes from the inside out?

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:58.520
Well, that's important because as it does
that, there's actually a change

00:31:58.550 --> 00:32:02.720
in that bulb region down below,
because normally, there's an interface,

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:06.200
there's a conversation that's occurring
between the stem cell population,

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.290
the melanocytes, and the hair itself,
and they support each other.

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:10.560
Remember, there's blood vessels going

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:15.050
into that area or rather capillaries
that are feeding that area as well.

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:19.520
After the catagen phase comes
the telogen phase, which means rest.

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:22.080
The telogen phase is a period in which no

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:25.780
new hair proteins are being
added by those stem cells.

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:27.400
During that telogen phase,

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:32.680
that bulb down there at the bottom,
instead of being nice and oval and really

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:35.280
having a lot of space in it with all these
different things like stem cells,

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:40.120
starts to pinch off
from the little tube that comes  down

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:42.840
from the surface  of the skin
that normally would have a hair in it.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.240
It starts to pinch off.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:46.440
At some point,  many hair follicles pinch

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:49.860
off that bulb region,
and it recedes and dies.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.320
When it recedes and dies,

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:54.940
the stem cell population
and the melanocytes go with it.

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:57.960
In other words,
there is no longer a population of stem

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:03.090
cells to give rise to more hair
after that telogen phase.

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:06.120
There's no longer melanocytes to pigment
the hair, and in fact,

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.880
the hair isn't there anymore,
so there's no hair even to pigment,

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:15.200
after that telogen phase, unless
it's a hair of a particular type such as

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:19.160
the hair on your scalp,
which can then reenter the cell cycle,

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:24.460
and get back into an anagen phase
and regrow more hair from stem cells.

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:26.160
There are three critical stages

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:30.200
of the life cycle of a hair that are
relevant to today's conversation.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.760
There's the anagen phase during
which the hair grows,

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:38.020
there's the catagen phase during which the
hair actually starts to recede and die.

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:41.700
The protein is actually
disappearing from the bottom up.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:43.240
Then we have the telogen phase,

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:46.920
which is the phase in which the stem cell
population is what's called a semi

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:52.740
quiescent, semiquiet, or completely
quiescent,  where it's not active at all.

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:55.800
Those three phases make up the life cycle

00:33:55.830 --> 00:34:01.480
of a hair, keeping in mind that for some
hairs, they can reenter the life cycle

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:05.360
and go back into the anagen phase,
if there's stem cells there,

00:34:05.390 --> 00:34:10.440
and if there is oxygen there,
and if there is sufficient blood support,

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:15.800
and—and this is a very important and— if
there are the appropriate  hormonal

00:34:15.830 --> 00:34:20.720
signals to support growth,
and there is a reduction or an absence

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:25.120
in the hormone  signals that actually
trigger that telogen phase.

00:34:25.130 --> 00:34:28.680
I make this point now because much
of the rest of today's discussion is

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:35.360
going to focus  on why particular hormones
such  as dihydrotestosterone cause hair

00:34:35.390 --> 00:34:39.680
loss and why inhibiting things  like
dihydrotestosterone can support

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:43.210
the preservation of hair  that you have
and the regrowth of hair.

00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:45.840
To make a long story very short,
and then we'll get into some additional

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:48.920
details that are relevant,
and that I hope you'll stick around

00:34:48.950 --> 00:34:54.380
to listen to, dihydrotestosterone,
which is a derivative  of testosterone,

00:34:54.410 --> 00:34:59.280
causes changes in that bulb region
where the stem cells reside.

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:02.680
It shortens or halt  the anagen phase

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:09.170
of hair growth, and it extends and
promotes the catagen and telogen phase.

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:12.640
When we hear that, "Oh
dihydrotestosterone makes your hair fall

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:16.620
out, or estrogen makes your hair grow,"
there are real chemical people,

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:20.160
or we should say biochemical
legitimate reasons as to why that is.

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:22.320
But it all comes back to this three

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.900
phases of hair growth: the anagen growth
phase, the catagen,

00:35:25.930 --> 00:35:29.400
catabolic or hair loss phase,
and the telogen phase,

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:33.040
which is a rest period in which the hairs
can either come back,

00:35:33.070 --> 00:35:36.280
if it reenters the anagen phase
or maybe it's over for good.

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:40.940
Hormones are the accelerator and the
brake on each one of those phases.

